    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW29vhgKfRxAMNLbYh7KprHvb)_\n\nBefore: there was no file tree in the config, so keeping the modules and the docs in `docs/tangles` in view meant switching to dired.\n\nAfter: pressing `<f9>` opens a treemacs side window rooted at `~/.config/emacs-modular` (`user-emacs-directory`), and pressing it again closes it. Nothing opens at startup.\n\nThe tree is rooted at a fixed folder, `sp-treemacs-root`, instead of treemacs' \"current project\", because the yadm repository's root is `$HOME` and following the project would show the whole home folder.\n\nHow: a new module, `libraries/modules/ui/sp-treemacs.el`, installs treemacs through Elpaca (`:ensure t`), binds `<f9>` to `sp-treemacs-toggle`, keeps treemacs' saved workspace in `sp-cache-dir`, and turns on `treemacs-follow-mode`. The toggle is defined in `:config` because it uses the `treemacs-is-path` macro. The module is added to the window management group in `init.el`, after `sp-mini-frame`. Its literate doc, `docs/tangles/sp-treemacs.org`, tangles back to the `.el` byte for byte (checked with `org-babel-tangle-file`), and has a `:tangle no` Sandbox block.\n\nTested in `emacs -nw -Q` with treemacs from source: `<f9>` is an autoload before first use, the first press adds the root and shows the tree, the second hides it, the third shows it again without adding the root twice.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01C3wkyoUfEEQ3ZAcMMvct4L)_",
